            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <windows />
                <VentilationSystem>

Please see my comments regarding the ventilation of your home within the "Additional Comments" section of this report.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

The domestic hot water for your house is currently supplied by a dedicated loop from the oil boiler, incorporating a 287 litre GSW storage tank. The hot water heating component is estimated to operate a 0.54 Efficiency Factor (EF) - approximately the annual percentage of heat that is delivered to the hot water verses what percentage goes out the chimney or is lost through short cycling inefficiencies of the boiler (especially during the summer months).



Please look at my upgrade recommendation to the current oil boiler. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE) - the average efficiency over the whole heating season, not just the peak efficiency. Using this boiler to also heat your domestic hot water (still using the GSW storage tank) will increase the hot water efficiency to 0.79 EF.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

The estimated annual savings have been incorporated into the savings of the boiler upgrade, so please have a look at that section of my report.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil boiler is an NTI direct vent unit that is estimated to be about 86.5% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE) - the average efficiency over the whole heating season, not just the peak efficiency. This boiler is also used to heat the domestic hot water, including the 287 litre GSW storage tank. The hot water heating component is estimated to operate at a 0.54 Efficiency Factor (EF) - approximately the annual percentage of heat that is delivered to the hot water verses what percentage goes up the chimney or is lost through short cycling inefficiencies of the boiler (especially during the summer months). The current boiler and hot water tank are located in the mechanical room accessed through the garage.



I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. Using this boiler to also heat your domestic hot water (still using the GSW storage tank) will increase the hot water efficiency to 0.79 EF.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,167&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs ($817 for heating &amp; $350 for hot water), decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;31.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;., and you would qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for the new boiler.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation.



As discussed while I was at your house, the Arctic Energy Alliance always recommends homeowners complete all energy conservation (eg: LED Bulbs, timer switches, etc.) &amp; energy efficiency (eg: heating &amp; hot water systems) measures before investing in renewable energy generation systems.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system on the rear South East facing roof of your house, flush mounted on the roof, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save about $ 915&lt;/b&gt; per year in electricity costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 2,500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system: 1/3 of purchase cost, up to a maximum of $2,500 per year in a hydro community. Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Ventilation&lt;/b&gt;



Your home has a lot of exhaust ventilation:



1. A kitchen range hood fan in the main residence and another in the secondary suite

2. A dryer for the main residence and another in the secondary suite.

3. A total of 5 bathroom exhaust fans (3 bathrooms + the hot tub in the main residence and another bathroom in the secondary suite).

4. A central vacuum that vents to the exterior of the main living area.



All of the above exhaust ventilation equipment does not recovery any heat from the outgoing air.



It appears the "make-up" air for the above-mentioned exhaust fans is managed by an outside "fresh air" supply connected to the Hi Velocity fancoils, which includes a pre-heat hydronic loop to heat the combined air mixture (return air from the house + the fresh air from outside) before the heated air is distributed evenly throughout the house.



The blower door air leakage test indicated that your house is very air tight, comparable with current high efficiency new housing construction in Yellowknife. As your house is fully insulated with spray polyurethane foam, we should expect the house to be quite air tight. When I operated the blower door, I was expecting to feel more air coming in through the supply air vents in the floors (from the outside make up air connected to the Hi Velocity units, however the flow coming in appeared to be low. As your house has a large interior heated volume (&gt;38,000 cu. ft.), and there are a lot of supply vents throughout the house, it was probably just a bit hard to "feel the air" coming in.



In new construction that is built air tight, almost all stick-built houses now include a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) system that integrates full house ventilation with heat recovery efficiency of 65% - 80% (heat recovered from the outgoing air &amp; transferred to the incoming air), with all bathroom ventilation integrated (with boost switches). Air tight houses will often have kitchen range hood fans that are recirculation (with charcoal filters) or vented out, with a dedicated make up air right at the stove. In very air tight houses, standard clothes dryers do not work well as they cannot obtain the 150 CFM of air flow through them ..... so homeowners end up with a pile of warm wet clothes at the end of the cycle. In these situations, homeowners will opt for a non-venting, condensing dryer.



Taking all of the above into account .............. your house is actually pretty well balanced and appears to be just slightly under-ventilated. In an ideal world, your house would have an HRV (to recover 65% - 75% of the heat from the outgoing exhaust air), however this is near impossible to effectively retrofit into your house now.



Bottom Line - just pay attention to how the house feels. If you are experiencing excess condensation on windows, then operate the exhaust fans a bit more or increase the amount of fresh air supply to the Hi Velocity units. If the house is too dry, than ventilate the house a bit less.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (both kitchen range hoods, all 5 bathroom fans, both dryers and the central vacuum indicated a pressure drop of 25.9 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment could cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current boiler is a direct-vent model and the wood stove has a dedicated combustion air supply, however it is susceptible to backdrafting combustion products into the house each time the door is opened to add more wood.



During my assessment of your home, there appeared to be functioning smoke or carbon monoxide (CO) detectors - please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;



The following Nov. 1, 2017 values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.12 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Your Energy Advisor&lt;/b&gt;



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



&lt;b&gt;Ken Baigent

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance&lt;/b&gt;

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



&lt;b&gt;Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!&lt;/b&gt;</AdditionalComments>
